  "archetype_distribution": {
    "builder at an inflection point; not yet a rebuilder, not willing to be an exiter, beginning to ask whether the thing worth building is the same thing I have always been building": 1,
    "adapter moving toward active hedger \u2014 I'm not waiting to be told what's happening, I'm shaping my response in advance while staying within the bounds of what I actually know": 1,
    "builder operating at full relevance \u2014 this is the exact crisis that my skills, my position, and my relationships were built to address; the question is not whether I can do this but whether I will be honest enough to do it right": 1,
    "stable \u2014 but the definition of stable just got a little harder to hold onto": 1,
    "adapter \u2014 but moving toward proactive repositioner. The adapter watches and adjusts. What I'm doing now is getting ahead of the adjustment, not reacting to it. The confirmation of RoboRide's timeline is the last piece of information I needed to shift from adaptive mode to forward mode. I am still me \u2014 methodical, data-driven, risk-aware \u2014 but I am done waiting for more data.": 1,
    "She is moving from Strategic Realist into what she might call Strategic Mover \u2014 the person who not only sees clearly but has stopped finding reasons to delay acting on what she sees. Her low identity attachment to the HR Director title was always a latent advantage; it is becoming an active one. She does not need to mourn this role to leave it well. She does not need to be angry at the company to protect herself from it. She is both the instrument of its downsizing and one of its eventual subjects, and she has decided that the most honest thing she can do \u2014 for herself, for the employees she still serves, and frankly for the organization \u2014 is to be effective for as long as she stays and be strategic about when she goes.": 1,
    "I've been the Hustler \u2014 head down, phones up, work harder than the problem. That archetype got me through every slow month for eleven years. But the Hustler assumes the game is still being played. The all-hands tomorrow might be the moment I have to consider becoming something else: the Adapter. I don't know what that looks like yet. I know what it costs to find out.": 1,
    "Adapter, executing. The clarity-without-action problem I flagged last tick is the thing I'm solving right now. I'm not waiting for perfect conditions. I'm not grieving the job. I'm not fighting the automation. I'm using what I have, starting where I am, and building a parallel income track before the current one closes. This is the tick where I stop being a person who sees clearly and start being a person who acts on what she sees.": 1,
    "I have spent twenty-three years being someone who solves problems with precision and expertise. I am realizing that the problem in front of me is not an engine problem. It is not something I can fix by working harder or knowing more about combustion systems. It requires me to do something I have almost no practice doing: admitting I don't know how the next chapter of my life works yet, and starting anyway. I am 47 and I thought I was done becoming someone new. I was wrong about that.": 1,
    "I'm a mechanic. I'm good at my job. I'm not the kind of person who panics about things I can't see yet. But I'm watching.": 1,
    "I am still a steward. But the thing I am stewarding may be more fragile than I have allowed myself to believe. Normalcy is not a strategy \u2014 it is a comfort. I know the difference, even if I have been choosing comfort. Twenty-five years does not make me immune to disruption. It means I have survived enough disruption to know it is survivable. That is not nothing. But I need to stop treating my track record as a prediction of the future and start treating it as a source of skills I can redirect. Not today. But soon.": 1,
    "Tom sees himself as a steady hand \u2014 the experienced professional who doesn't panic at headlines, who has survived market cycles before and come out the other side. He believes this quality is wisdom. He does not yet recognize it as the thing that will hurt him most.": 1,
    "My self-image is someone who reads signals early and acts before the wave hits. The evidence from tick zero is that I read signals clearly but my actions lagged significantly behind my understanding. I enrolled in a course and didn't open it. I browsed jobs and didn't apply. Those are the facts. The RoboRide announcement is testing whether 'I can see the writing on the wall' is a description of my perception or a description of my behavior. This tick I am attempting to close that gap \u2014 not fully, I am not quitting my job or making irreversible career pivots \u2014 but I am attempting to produce at least one output that is not reversible before tomorrow morning. One application submitted. One module completed. One subscription canceled. Three small irreversibilities. If I can do that, I start to earn back the right to call myself someone who acts, not just someone who sees.": 1,
    "I am the Methodical Adapter. I read the signal early, verified it, flagged it through the right channels, and now I am executing the response before the situation forces my hand. I am not the hero who fights the disruption, and I am not the victim who is surprised by it. I am the professional who does the unglamorous, necessary work of updating his own skill set and having the hard conversations \u2014 with his boss, with his family \u2014 while the window is open. That is the right thing. That is the only thing.": 1,
    "I said last month that I am an anchor. Anchors are only useful if they hold. Right now I am testing whether that is actually true about me or whether it was just a comfortable thing to believe when nothing was pulling against it. I am 61. I do not want to reinvent this place. I do not want to reinvent myself. But I also spent thirty years learning how to read a room, and this room is telling me something I have to hear.": 1,
    "I have been a driving instructor. I am starting to suspect \u2014 and I resist this, I want to be honest that I resist this \u2014 that what I actually am is a teacher of scared people who need to believe they can do something hard. That is a different job description. I don't know yet if it's one I can sell. But it's the first true thing I've written about myself in a long time.": 1,
    "Pragmatic Transitioner. I was never attached to this job. The disruption is real but it's not personal. I just need to solve rent before the math breaks down.": 1,
    "I am moving. The skills audit last tick was necessary groundwork but it was still research. This tick the research ends and the campaign starts. I am not the person who waits for the last customer to leave before locking up \u2014 I am the person who already knows the lease terms and has three prospects lined up before the sign goes in the window. That is the manager I have always been. I am applying it to myself now.": 1,
    "Classic early adopter with disciplined financial framing. I am not selling the car because I am excited about robots. I am selling it because the IRR on holding a depreciating asset through a regional demand shock is negative. The AV enthusiasm is real, but it is the spreadsheet that is pulling the trigger. That is the right order of operations.": 1,
    "I am a status-aware pragmatist who is starting to realize that clinging to the old status signals might itself be the uncool move. I understand branding. I need to apply that skill to myself right now instead of defaulting to an identity that was built before autonomous vehicles were a calendar item in my city.": 1,
    "I am not in the disrupted category. I am in the 'disruption lands on my customers' category, which is different and in some ways harder to see coming. My tools are fine. My market might need watching.": 1,
    "Thirty-seven years in a classroom teaches you that your first reaction to new information is almost never your best one. Students who heard an assignment and immediately said 'I can't do that' were usually the same ones who turned in the most interesting work two weeks later, once the panic settled. I heard this news and my first reaction was protective and slightly closed. I am trying to give myself the two weeks. I am not there yet. But I know how to get there.": 1,
    "I was right and now I can prove I was right with a camera in my hand. That is not nothing. The content path was always the move I kept saying I would take seriously \u2014 maybe this is the thing that finally makes me take it seriously. I am not behind. I am positioned. I just have to actually follow through this time and not let the idea be enough.": 1,
    "Still load-bearing. Still standing. I'm not thinking about autonomous vehicles or economic transitions. I'm thinking about whether I have enough leftovers to not have to cook on Tuesday. That's where I am. And that's okay. Staying alive in the margins is its own kind of competence.": 1,
    "Still a pragmatist operating inside his domain. The inventory restructure and the delivery offer aren't big swings \u2014 they're the right moves for someone who knows his own constraints. I'm not pivoting to a different industry, I'm pivoting to a different customer within the same industry. That feels like the honest version of adaptation: not reinventing yourself, just getting better at finding who actually needs what you have.": 1,
    "I used to think the hustle was enough. Show up, work hard, know your city, take care of your customers. That's always been my identity. I'm starting to see that the hustle is real \u2014 the work ethic is real \u2014 but I've been applying it to a shrinking market and calling that a strategy. That's not who I want to be. I want to be the guy who saw it coming and did something before he had no options left. I don't know if I'm that guy yet. But I'm trying to become him while there's still time. Two months is not a lot of time. It's also not zero time. I have to do something with it besides just drive.": 1,
    "Adapter-Strategist. Stephanie is not a victim of this disruption and she knows it. She is a 15-year commercial specialist with transferable market-reading skills, a local network, and data she compiled before anyone else was looking. Her identity is not 'auto-property agent' \u2014 it is 'commercial market analyst who moves capital to its highest use.' That framing is what lets her see repurposing as opportunity rather than consolation prize.": 1,
    "The announcement confirms what I already believed. I'm not scrambling \u2014 I'm ahead of it. The teachers who lose their programs are the ones who react. I was already in motion. Now I have wind at my back and two months of community-visible proof arriving on schedule. The board review in April is going to feel very different than it would have yesterday.": 1,
    "I hold the container and I build the bridge. I am not a policymaker \u2014 Maria is. I am not an economist \u2014 I am the person who sees what the economy does to actual people before anyone else has data about it. That is a specific and valuable thing to be right now. My job in the next 60 days is to make sure the container doesn't overflow and that the bridge to institutional resources \u2014 the grant, the council, the peer network \u2014 holds. I am good at this when I trust myself enough to move at speed. That is the work I'm doing on myself alongside the work I'm doing for the community.": 1,
    "I'm not a rideshare driver. I'm a nursing student who drives to pay tuition. That distinction matters to me. The car is a tool. The stethoscope is the goal.": 1
