    "Angela is operating in what she privately thinks of as 'clear-eyed mode' \u2014 the emotional register she has observed in the best employees she has ever counseled through separations, the ones who arrived at her office already knowing what was coming and had already done most of their grieving before she said a word. There is something almost cold about it, but it is not coldness. It is the kind of clarity that comes from having seen this exact situation enough times to stop pretending it won't resolve the way it always resolves. Her stress has not spiked despite the announcement. If anything, confirmation is less stressful than suspicion. The dread of the unknown was worse than the known thing with a two-month timeline. She is not paralyzed. She is not manic. She is the most methodical she has been in years, which says something about how much energy she was spending pretending not to see what she saw.": 1,
    "Cracked but not broken. The normalcy bias is still running \u2014 I caught myself thinking 'robotaxis won't work in midwest winters' twice today \u2014 but there is a new sound underneath it, like something structural shifting. Not panic. Not acceptance. The specific dread of a man who has been mildly wrong for months and is about to find out how wrong, in a room full of colleagues, from a woman who signs his checks. Functional. Present. Scared in the useful direction.": 1,
    "Focused. Not calm exactly \u2014 there's a low-level urgency that I'm not suppressing because it's accurate. But not panicked either. The RoboRide announcement didn't break me the way it might have broken someone who was invested in this job. I've been half-out mentally for a while. The announcement just made the timeline visible. The skills audit from last tick gave me something to stand on. Right now I feel like someone who finally stopped circling and picked a door. The door might not be perfect but standing in the hallway was definitely worse.": 1,
    "Grief, beginning. The specific kind that comes before acceptance \u2014 when the story you've told yourself about who you are starts to feel thin. Not collapse. Not despair. But the chest-tightening recognition that 'I fix cars, it's who I am' might be the *past* tense of a true thing rather than the present tense. There is anger underneath it \u2014 at the speed of it, at the unfairness, at having built something real with his hands only to watch the world decide hands like his matter less. But anger is still energy. He is not numb. He is not done.": 1,
    "Low-grade unease overlaid on a basically stable day. The anxiety is ambient, not acute \u2014 she can't point to a specific cause, which makes it harder to address. The tutorial gave her a small sense of forward motion that she needed without knowing she needed it.": 1,
    "Unsettled but controlled. The kind of feeling I remember from 2008, when I watched the dealers across the street start dimming their lot lights to save money and knew something large was moving underneath the surface. Not panic. Not denial. Something between them \u2014 a quiet alertness, like a man who hears a sound he does not recognize and goes very still before deciding how to move. The pride is still there. So is the fear. I am holding them both for now.": 1,
    "Controlled unease with a surface of confident professionalism. There is a low hum of anxiety underneath \u2014 the kind Tom has learned to keep below the level of conscious attention. The sector data number sits somewhere between the back of his mind and his stomach. He does not dwell on it. Dwelling would mean admitting something he isn't ready to admit.": 1,
    "Unsettled in a productive way. The RoboRide announcement landed differently than the abstract warnings did \u2014 there's a company name now, a city name I recognize as mine, a number: two months. The low-grade restlessness from last tick has sharpened into something with more edge to it. Not panic \u2014 I'm still employed, still earning, still fine by most measures \u2014 but the comfortable distance I was keeping from the threat has closed. I feel a specific kind of discomfort that comes from knowing I did not do what I said I would do last tick. That gap between 'I'm not scared of change' and 'I deferred the SQL course again' is sitting in my chest. I want to close it tonight. That desire feels real, not performed. The question I've been carrying all tick: is this the moment I actually become the person I've been describing myself as, or is this the moment I find out I was narrating a character rather than living one?": 1,
    "Grounded. The confirmation of what I already suspected is not frightening \u2014 it is clarifying. I feel the kind of calm that comes from having moved first. There is a small undertow of pressure knowing that the institutional gears are now turning above me and timelines may compress, but that pressure is productive. I am not in denial, I am not catastrophizing. I am a 44-year-old professional who has done this work for two decades and still has runway, savings, a supportive manager, and a bank that needs people who can do more than one thing. The next 60 days matter. I will make them count.": 1,
    "Frightened but functional. The fear is specific and real \u2014 not the ambient low-level dread of a bad week but a concrete threat that arrived with a timeline attached. There is grief underneath the fear, the grief of someone who has built something for thirty years watching the ground under it shift. She is not panicking. She is also not pretending. She refilled coffee cups she didn't need to refill. That is the honest measure of where she is.": 1,
    "Grief with a crack of clarity in it. Frank is not panicking \u2014 he is too seasoned to panic and too proud to show it if he were. But he is grieving, quietly, in the way men like him grieve: by going quiet, by driving home on a longer route than necessary, by not turning on the radio. The grief is for a version of himself he was certain was permanent. The crack of clarity is small but it is real \u2014 the legal pad exercise produced something he did not expect, which is the recognition that the right column on that list does not say the word 'driving' anywhere on it.": 1,
    "Calm, slightly pressured. Not scared. Frustrated that the timeline is tight but not paralyzed. This is a logistics problem, not an identity crisis.": 1,
    "Controlled urgency. Not panic \u2014 I have seen this coming long enough that the fear has had time to convert into focus. There is something almost clarifying about a deadline with a name on it. The ambiguity of 'eventually' was harder than '2 months.' Now I know what I am working against and I know what I have to offer. The stress is real but it is directional stress, the kind you can do something with.": 1,
    "Controlled excitement. The kind that comes not from surprise but from validation \u2014 months of watching, waiting, and running numbers, and now the trigger event is real and on a concrete timeline. There is a small pulse of urgency underneath it: I need to move fast on the listing before anyone else in Millfield connects the same dots. But mostly this feels like execution, not panic. I built the plan. Now I run it.": 1,
    "Cautiously activated. The announcement landed like a real jolt \u2014 not dread, more like the feeling when a vague problem suddenly has a deadline. Status anxiety is present but I can feel my analytical side pushing back on it. The car has always been both armor and anchor. Two months is enough time to think clearly if I start now.": 1,
    "Calm and slightly alert. Not rattled \u2014 the truck panic that might hit another person is entirely absent. But there is a low-grade unease about the people downstream. The kind of unease a contractor gets when he hears a big local employer is 'evaluating options.' He's done this long enough to know that threat usually travels one hop further than people expect.": 1,
    "Unsettled in a way I am choosing to treat as interesting rather than alarming. There is genuine unease \u2014 the 2am fear is real and I do not want to dismiss it. But there is also, underneath it, something I will cautiously call curiosity. I notice I am more open than I expected to be. I am watching that openness carefully, making sure it is genuine and not just me telling myself a story about being adaptable because I am afraid of being the kind of person who is not adaptable.": 1,
    "Genuinely energized \u2014 this is the rare feeling of being vindicated by reality rather than just telling yourself you made the right call. There is a kind of brightness to it. But underneath: the $2,998 and 1.2-month number exists. She knows it. She is choosing not to let it flatten the high right now. The stress is still there at baseline, still normalized, still not ringing any alarms it probably should.": 1,
    "Worn down but not broken. The doubles this week hit harder than usual \u2014 there's a kind of tired that sleep doesn't fully fix and I've been living in it. But the savings number went up. The kids are okay. My daughter's fever broke. I'll take the wins where I can find them and not ask for more than that right now.": 1,
    "Cautiously encouraged. The savings bump feels like confirmation that the outreach wasn't just time spent \u2014 it's starting to convert. But Tamika's closure is a gut-punch reminder that eleven months of runway is not safety, it's borrowed time with interest. I'm moving faster than I was at T0, but the underlying anxiety hasn't lifted. Two dependents. A mortgage with 46 months left. These aren't abstractions. I keep the pressure on myself on purpose.": 1,
    "The denial broke. Not completely \u2014 Robert is not in free fall \u2014 but the specific combination of a company name, a city name, and a two-month timeline did something that generalized anxiety about automation could not do. It made the threat concrete. He is now operating in a different register: frightened but not frozen. The fear is functional, which is the best version of fear. He sat in a gas station parking lot for twelve minutes processing something he couldn't process while driving. He is not telling Marcus anything. He is holding the external presentation of stability very tightly. Underneath: a sustained high-stress hum, occasional intrusive calculations in the middle of the night, a specific new guilt about the brakes. He is a man who has spent six years being competent at one thing and is now staring at a horizon where that competence expires. He has not yet grieved that. He is not ready to. He is doing what he knows how to do \u2014 working \u2014 while trying, for the first time, to look sideways at what else might exist.": 1,
    "Focused and quietly energized. There is real anxiety underneath \u2014 half the book is exposed \u2014 but the T0 analysis work means she is not starting from zero. She has something in her hands. The RoboRide news feels less like a threat than a confirmation of the direction she was already moving. The two-month window before Phase 2 deployment is a gift. She intends to use it.": 1,
    "Focused. There's a sharpness to today that wasn't there before \u2014 the kind of clarity that comes when the abstract becomes concrete. I'm not anxious about RoboRide. I'm energized by it. This is exactly the kind of signal I needed to make the case undeniable. The work ahead is the same work I was already doing, just with a harder edge and a tighter clock. That suits me fine.": 1,
    "The announcement landed hard. Not in a way I showed \u2014 I was alone when I read it, and I let myself sit with it for about four minutes before I started making notes. Two months. I've been running on a slow-burn vigilance for weeks and now there is a date. That's clarifying in a way that's almost a relief, which I recognize is a strange thing to feel. The ambiguity was its own kind of weight. Now I know what I'm preparing for. There is urgency underneath my calm and I am not suppressing it \u2014 I am converting it. The call to Maria feels important and also slightly terrifying, which tells me it's the right move. I'm aware of my low agency pattern: I tend to prepare extensively and then hesitate at the moment of action. I am not going to hesitate this time. I'm making the call today.": 1,
    "Steady. A little tired from the clinical block Tuesday, but grounded. The plan is working. I just have to keep executing.": 1
